    "q-68170": {
      "dom_id": "step-q68170",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Rick is driving on a highway. The relationship between the time $t$ (in minutes) he drives and the distance $d$ (in miles) he covers is shown in the table below.\n\n| Minutes ($t$) | Miles ($d$) |\n| --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $3$ |\n| $8$ | $6$ |\n| $12$ | $9$ |\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. There is **not** a proportional relationship between $d$ and $t.$\n\nII. There is a proportional relationship between $d$ and $t.$\n\nIII. The constant of proportionality is $\\frac{3}{4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's add a new column to our table and compute the ratio of miles per hour for each row. \n\n| Minutes ($t$) | Miles ($d$) | Miles / Minutes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $4$ | $3$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $8$ | $6$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n| $12$ | $9$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ |\n\n \n\nThe ratio of miles per minute is the same for each row. Therefore, there is a proportional relationship between $d$ and $t$  and the  constant of proportionality is $k=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
